# {{ model.assembly_name }}

A .NET 10 class library around the Rust crate **{{ model.crate_name }}** {{ model.crate_version }}: the crate's native
library for the runtime identifiers listed below, and a C# API over it.

## How it was made

Generated by CodeBrix.RustTools.CrateBuilder from {{ model.source_description }}.
{{~ if model.is_pass_through ~}}
The crate already exports C functions; this wrapper declares each of them one-to-one and
forwards to it from a static method of `{{ model.lib_class_name }}`.
{{~ else ~}}
CrateBuilder generated a small C facade crate over the crate's public API, built it for each
target, and generated the C# types and methods below from the same mapping plan. Errors become
exceptions, Rust objects become disposable classes, plain Rust structs become value types.
{{~ end ~}}

Do not edit the generated files: regenerating the wrapper rewrites them (generated-files.json lists
them). Every generated type is `partial`, so additions go in files of your own.

## Native libraries included

| Runtime identifier | File |
|--------------------|------|
{{~ for runtime in model.runtimes ~}}
| {{ runtime.rid }} | runtimes/{{ runtime.rid }}/native/{{ runtime.file_name }}{{ if runtime.note }} ({{ runtime.note }}){{ end }} |
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if model.runtimes.empty? ~}}
| (none) | no target was compiled |
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if !model.missing_targets.empty? ~}}

Not included (requested, but not built):

{{~ for missing in model.missing_targets ~}}
- {{ missing.rid }}: {{ missing.reason }}
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ end ~}}

At run time the library is loaded from `runtimes/<rid>/native/` next to the application (the
build copies it there), then from the application folder, then from the system's library path.
On any other machine the first call throws `DllNotFoundException` naming the expected path.

## Using it

Reference the project from your application:

```xml
<ItemGroup>
  <ProjectReference Include="path/to/{{ model.assembly_name }}/{{ model.assembly_name }}.csproj" />
</ItemGroup>
```
{{~ if model.is_packable ~}}

or pack it (`dotnet pack -c Release -o <feed folder>`), add that folder as a package source, and
reference the package. Every pack gets a new date-stamped version (1.<years>.<day>.<minute>, UTC);
use the version in the name of the .nupkg file:

```xml
<ItemGroup>
  <PackageReference Include="{{ model.packaging.package_id }}" Version="<version of the .nupkg>" />
</ItemGroup>
```

The package holds the same native files under `runtimes/<rid>/native/`, and NuGet copies them to
the application's output the same way.
{{~ end ~}}

```csharp
using {{ model.root_namespace }};

{{~ if model.first_entry_method_name ~}}
// The crate's free functions are static methods of {{ model.lib_class_name }}, for example
// {{ model.first_entry_signature }}
{{ model.lib_class_name }}.{{ model.first_entry_method_name }}(/* ... */);
{{~ else ~}}
// The entry class (static) is {{ model.lib_class_name }}.
{{~ end ~}}
```
{{~ if model.lib_class_note ~}}

{{ model.lib_class_note }}
{{~ end ~}}

## Rules

{{~ if !model.handle_names.empty? ~}}
- **Dispose what you create.** {{ for name in model.handle_names }}`{{ name }}`{{ if !for.last }}, {{ end }}{{ end }} own native objects:
  use `using` or call `Dispose()` (calling it twice is harmless). After `Dispose()` every other call
  throws `ObjectDisposedException`. A method marked as consuming in its documentation uses the
  object up; afterwards every call except `Dispose()` throws `InvalidOperationException`.
- **One thread at a time per object.** The native objects are not thread-safe; calls on one
  instance are serialised by a lock, so share an instance across threads only if waiting is fine.
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if !model.is_pass_through ~}}
- **Strings are UTF-8.** Text goes to the crate as UTF-8 with an explicit length; text that comes back
  is decoded from UTF-8.
- **Errors are exceptions.** A Rust `Result` error, a rejected argument or a panic inside the crate
  throws `{{ model.exception_class_name }}`; its message is the Rust text and its `Status` says which:
  -1 a null argument, -2 an argument the native side rejected (a length, invalid UTF-8), -3 an error
  the crate returned, -4 a panic inside the crate (an object that panicked is broken: only
  `Dispose()` is allowed afterwards), -5 an object already used up by a consuming method.
{{~ end ~}}
{{~ if model.links_web_gpu ~}}
- **WebGPU.** The crate links the CodeBrix WebGPU engine; the wrapper references
  CodeBrix.WebGpu.Native and loads it before the crate's library.
{{~ end ~}}

